What:		/proc/process_info
Date:		October 2026
Contact:		Custom Android kernel maintainer
Description:
		CONFIG_PROCESS_PUBLIC_CONTROL creates this read-only 0444 task
		metadata interface. All users can list all tasks visible in the
		PID namespace captured at open, regardless of UID or hidepid.
		Normal Android apps see the system's shared PID namespace.

		The first line names tab-separated version-1 columns:
		tid tgid ppid uid state policy nice rt_priority cpu start_ns
		group_start_ns kernel comm argv0

		Each following line represents one thread, including leaders
		(tid == tgid) and visible kernel threads (kernel == 1). UID is the
		initial user namespace Linux UID. CPU is the sampled current/last
		CPU, not an affinity mask. State is the usual single-letter task
		state; policy is the Linux numeric scheduler policy.

		start_ns is the task's raw start_boottime in nanoseconds; the
		group_start_ns token belongs to its group leader. Treat tokens as
		opaque unsigned 64-bit decimal integers, not floating point values.
		comm is at most TASK_COMM_LEN-1 bytes. argv0 is bounded to 256
		bytes and ends at its first NUL; other arguments and environment
		are not exported. Unavailable argv0 is empty. Tabs, CR, LF and
		backslashes in strings are escaped as backslash plus three octal
		digits. Names can contain truncated/non-UTF-8 bytes; decode safely.

		Enumeration is a live walk, not an atomic full-system snapshot.
		Tasks may exit, appear or change fields during reads. Kernel task
		and PID-namespace references protect iterator lifetime; no RCU
		lock is held across mm access. No register or kernel address data
		is provided. This node does not grant access to other /proc files.

What:		/proc/process_ctl
Date:		October 2026
Contact:		Custom Android kernel maintainer
Description:
		This 0666 node lets any application change nice across UIDs,
		without root, capabilities, or a UID allowlist. One complete
		command per write, at most 128 bytes:

		  thread TID START_NS NICE
		  process TGID GROUP_START_NS NICE

		NICE must be -20..19 (smaller gives greater scheduling weight).
		Use tokens from process_info. Wrong tokens return ESTALE; absent
		tasks return ESRCH. IDs are resolved in the PID namespace captured
		when this file was opened. A process command requires a leader ID
		and modifies a snapshot of its live threads, up to 4096 threads.
		Groups growing beyond the allocated snapshot return E2BIG and
		can be retried. New threads after collection are not guaranteed
		to be included. Targets are referenced before any scheduling call.

		Only userspace SCHED_NORMAL/SCHED_BATCH tasks are supported.
		Kernel threads and other policies return EOPNOTSUPP (a concurrent
		policy change may instead cause EINVAL/EAGAIN). The internal
		scheduler API preserves policy/reset-on-fork and user-defined
		utilization clamps; non-user-defined clamps follow its defaults.
		UID/capability checks are delegated by this interface only; the
		normal scheduler syscalls and SELinux task policy are unchanged.

		Success returns write size. Errors may occur after a process
		command partially succeeded; there is no rollback. Seek the same
		open read/write descriptor to 0 and read:
		  updated=N error=ERRNO
		This reports the last parsed operation for that descriptor, counting
		successful thread operations, including already-satisfied values.
		Parsing/copy errors leave the last parsed-operation report intact.
		A new descriptor starts with updated=0 error=0. Refresh process_info
		to inspect actual state after a partial error or concurrent writer.

		Changes are one-shot; Android/system scheduling policy can later
		change nice again. This does not force foreground status, oom_adj,
		cgroups, CPU affinity, frequency, real-time priority or CPU share.
		SELinux read/write/getattr/zero-length O_TRUNC exemptions use only
		the two private proc inode markers. Other files and enforcing mode
		are not changed. Namespace/mount visibility and other LSMs apply.
